/tr><tr><th scope="row"><a href="/submarkets/north-myrtle-beach/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">North Myrtle Beach</a></th><td><b>$535,000</b><span><i class="tt-d bal" aria-hidden="true"></i>6.1 months</span></td><td><b>$300,000</b><span><i class="tt-d buy" aria-hidden="true"></i>7.5 months</span></td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><a href="/submarkets/surfside-beach/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Surfside Beach</a></th><td><b>$620,000</b><span><i class="tt-d sell" aria-hidden="true"></i>3.6 months</span></td><td><span>Too few sales</span></td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><a href="/submarkets/garden-city/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Garden City</a></th><td><b>$385,000</b><span><i class="tt-d buy" aria-hidden="true"></i>9.4 months</span></td><td><b>$220,000</b><span><i class="tt-d bal" aria-hidden="true"></i>5.1 months</span></td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><a href="/submarkets/little-river/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Little River</a></th><td><b>$350,000</b><span><i class="tt-d sell" aria-hidden="true"></i>3.7 months</span></td><td><b>$155,000</b><span><i class="tt-d buy" aria-hidden="true"></i>8.1 months</span></td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><a href="/submarkets/carolina-forest/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Carolina Forest</a></th><td><b>$395,000</b><span><i class="tt-d sell" aria-hidden="true"></i>3.7 months</span></td><td><span>Too few sales</span></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Forestbrook</th><td><b>$331,000</b><span><i class="tt-d sell" aria-hidden="true"></i>3.1 months</span></td><td><span>Too few sales</span></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Socastee</th><td><b>$292,000</b><span><i class="tt-d sell" aria-hidden="true"></i>4.0 months</span></td><td><span>Too few sales</span></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Red Hill</th><td><b>$310,000</b><span><i class="tt-d sell" aria-hidden="true"></i>3.7 months</span></td><td><span>Too few sales</span></td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><a href="/submarkets/conway/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Conway</a></th><td><b>$265,000</b><span><i class="tt-d sell" aria-hidden="true"></i>4.1 months</span></td><td><span>Too few sales</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Garden City has the most supply of houses, about 9 months, but more than half of them are manufactured homes. "Too few sales" marks a town with too few resales of that kind to count.</p><div class="bx"><p class="bx-t">How much room is there on the home you like?</p><p>Send us the address. An agent pulls the nearby sales and how long homes like it have sat on the market, so you know where to start your offer.</p><p class="bx-b"><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/" data-topic="buying-in-myrtle-beach-offer">Have us price one home</a></p></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="prices"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="What homes cost">How much does a house or condo cost in Myrtle Beach?</h2><div class="c3b-side"><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The middle resale house or other home in Horry County sold for about $350,000 in the past year, and the middle resale condo for about $215,000. Half sold for more, half for less.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem"><a href="/buyers/new-construction/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">New homes</a> are a big part of the market: builders sold more than 1 in 4 of the homes recorded in the county. The middle home a builder sold, condos aside, went for about $330,000. New houses have the fewest months of supply of any kind of home here, about 3.</p><div class="c3-aerial"><figure class="c3ph-hero" style=""><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/2026-11-a/buyers-buying-in-myrtle-beach/carolina-forest-houses-and-ponds-from-above-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/2026-11-a/buyers-buying-in-myrtle-beach/carolina-forest-houses-and-ponds-from-above-600w.webp 600w, /images/2026-11-a/buyers-buying-in-myrtle-beach/carolina-forest-houses-and-ponds-from-above-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1136px" width="1200" height="900" alt="Rows of houses with gray roofs along curving streets around two ponds with fountains, with a shared pool and pine woods at the bottom, seen from above" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/USCODE-2023-title17/html/USCODE-2023-title17-chap1-sec105.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="U.S. government aerial photo. License: Public domain" title="License: Public domain">U.S. government aerial photo</a></span></div><figcaption>Houses around ponds in Carolina Forest, outside city limits, seen from above. The middle resale house there sold for about $395,000.</figcaption></figure></div><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What does a home cost each year besides the mortgage?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The middle house, about $350,000, costs about $7,850 a year besides the mortgage if you live in it outside city limits. That is about $650 a month. The biggest parts are upkeep, about $3,270, and insurance, about $2,000.</p></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The middle resale condo, about $215,000, costs about $10,170 a year, and three quarters of that is the condo fee. The fee pays to run and keep up the shared building and grounds, and may pay the building's insurance.</p><style>main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact{padding-top:1rem;padding-bottom:.85rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .cap{margin-bottom:.7rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.1rem .9rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowheader]{line-height:1.3}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .b{height:.5rem;min-width:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .v{line-height:1.25}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact figcaption{margin-top:.8rem}@media (max-width:699px){main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.45rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.05rem}}</style><figure class="c3-gchart c3-gchart-compact"><div role="table" aria-label="Yearly cost to own the middle condo or house, not counting the mortgage"><span class="cap" aria-hidden="true">Yearly cost to own the middle condo or house, not counting the mortgage</span><div role="rowgroup" class="sr"><div role="row"><span role="columnheader">Home, middle resale price</span><span role="columnheader">yearly cost</span></div></div><div role="rowgroup"><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Condo in Myrtle Beach</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">middle price $172,000</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.87" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$11,490</span></span></div><div role="row" class="ref"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">House in Myrtle Beach</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">middle price $430,000</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.621" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$8,210</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Condo in North Myrtle Beach</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">middle price $300,000</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.851" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$11,240</span></span></div><div role="row" class="ref"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">House in North Myrtle Beach</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">middle price $535,000</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.581" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$7,670</span></span></div></div></div><figcaption>For an owner who lives in the home: property tax, the county fee where it applies, insurance, the HOA or condo fee and upkeep.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">In Myrtle Beach and North Myrtle Beach the middle condo <a href="/buyers/cost-to-own/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">costs more to own each year</a> than the middle house. But it costs less to buy: in Myrtle Beach, less than half as much. Weigh the smaller loan against the larger fee.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="steps"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="The steps">What are the steps to buy a house in South Carolina?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Eight steps, from choosing an agent to the deed recorded in Conway. The biggest difference from many states: every South Carolina home closing must be supervised by a South Carolina attorney.</p><style>main#main .bs{list-style:none;margin:1.2rem 0 1.4rem;padding:0;max-width:760px;position:relative}main#main .bs::before{content:"";position:absolute;left:1.05rem;top:.6rem;bottom:.6rem;width:2px;background:rgba(28,32,40,.16)}main#main .bs>li{position:relative;margin:0 0 .7rem;padding:0 0 0 3.1rem}main#main .bs .n{position:absolute;left:0;top:.75rem;display:inline-flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;width:2.15rem;height:2.15rem;border-radius:50%;background:var(--navy);color:#fff;font-family:var(--sans);font-weight:700;font-size:.95rem}main#main .bs li.hot .n{background:#c4783a}main#main .bs .c{display:block;padding:.85rem 1rem .9rem;border-radius:16px;background:rgba(255,255,255,.62);border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.8);box-shadow:0 10px 26px -20px rgba(28,32,40,.35);color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6}main#main .bs li.hot .c{border:2px solid #c4783a;background:#fff}main#main .bs h3{display:block;color:var(--navy)!important;font-family:var(--sans)!important;font-size:1.1rem!important;line-height:1.35;margin:0 0 .3rem!important;font-weight:700;text-wrap:balance}main#main .bs p{margin:0 0 .45rem;max-width:none}main#main .bs p:last-child{margin-bottom:0}main#main .bs .sr{position:absolute;width:1px;height:1px;overflow:hidden;clip:rect(0 0 0 0);white-space:nowrap}@media (max-width:599px){main#main .bs::before{display:none}main#main .bs>li{padding:0}main#main .bs .n{left:.9rem;top:.8rem;width:1.9rem;height:1.9rem;font-size:.9rem}main#main .bs h3{display:flex!important;align-items:center;min-height:1.9rem;padding-left:2.5rem}main#main .bs .c{padding:.8rem .95rem .85rem}}</style><ol class="bs"><li><div class="c"><h3><span class="n">1</span><span class="sr"> Step 1: </span>Sign a buyer agreement with your agent</h3><p>In South Carolina, no firm represents you as your agent until you both sign a written agreement that says so. We sign it with you before your first tour.</p><p>It states what your agent is paid. That fee is negotiable, and a seller may still offer to pay it.</p></div></li><li><div class="c"><h3><span class="n">2</span><span class="sr"> Step 2: </span>Choose a home and make an offer</h3><p>Before you offer, we find the home's flood zone and get an insurance quote for the address, if you buy with Chapter3. You can sign the offer by e-signature from anywhere; South Carolina law counts it the same as ink.</p></div></li><li><div class="c"><h3><span class="n">3</span><span class="sr"> Step 3: </span>Inspect the home and read the papers</h3><p>In South Carolina, inspecting the home is the buyer's job. We line up the inspection.</p><p>The seller of most resale houses must give you the state's disclosure form before you sign. For a condo, we get the HOA papers and the rental rules.</p></div></li><li><div class="c"><h3><span class="n">4</span><span class="sr"> Step 4: </span>The loan and the appraisal</h3><p>Within three business days of your application, your lender must send a Loan Estimate, a form that lists the loan's terms and costs.</p><p>You get a free copy of the appraisal as soon as it is done.</p></div></li><li class="hot"><div class="c"><h3><span class="n">5</span><span class="sr"> Step 5: </span>The closing attorney</h3><p>A South Carolina attorney supervises the title search, the loan papers, the closing and the recording of the deed. With a home loan for personal or family use, the lender must ask which attorney you want and follow your choice.</p><p>We work with several closing attorneys and can recommend them once we are working with you. At least three business days before closing, your lender sends the Closing Disclosure: the loan's final terms and costs.</p></div></li><li><div class="c"><h3><span class="n">6</span><span class="sr"> Step 6: </span>Closing, here or from home</h3><p>You can sign at the attorney's office. From another state, you can sign the deed papers and the mortgage before a notary where you live, with two witnesses.</p></div></li><li><div class="c"><h3><span class="n">7</span><span class="sr"> Step 7: </span>The deed is recorded in Conway</h3><p>The deed and mortgage are recorded at the Horry County Register of Deeds in Conway. The seller pays the state's deed fee.</p><p><a href="/buyers/closing-costs/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">What the buyer pays at closing</a> is listed fee by fee on its own page.</p></div></li><li class="hot"><div class="c"><h3><span class="n">8</span><span class="sr"> Step 8: </span>Apply for the 4&nbsp;percent tax rate</h3><p>A home you live in and apply for is taxed on 4 percent of its value; a second home or a rental on 6 percent. The 4 percent rate is not automatic: you <a href="/buyers/property-taxes/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">apply to the county</a> after you buy.</p><p>On a house the county values at $350,000 outside the cities, the current tax is about $1,290 a year at 4 percent and about $4,220 at 6 percent. A sale resets the county's value, so the seller's tax bill is not yours.</p></div></li></ol><div class="bx"><p class="bx-t">Buying from another state?</p><p>Tell us where you live and what you want. We line up the inspection, the insurance quote and a closing attorney.</p><p class="bx-b"><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/" data-topic="buying-in-myrtle-beach-steps">Ask us to plan your purchase</a></p></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="out-of-state"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Buying from out of state">Can I buy a home in Myrtle Beach from out of state?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes, and many buyers do:</p><style>main#main .bo{list-style:none;margin:1.1rem 0 1.3rem;padding:0;display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(2,minmax(0,1fr));gap:.65rem;max-width:760px}main#main .bo li{margin:0;padding:1rem 1rem .95rem;border-radius:18px;background:linear-gradient(160deg,rgba(42,48,64,.96),rgba(28,32,40,.98));color:rgba(244,239,232,.92);line-height:1.45;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:1rem}main#main .bo strong{display:block;color:#d4894a;font-family:var(--serif);font-size:clamp(1.7rem,6.5vw,2.2rem);line-height:1.05;margin-bottom:.35rem;font-weight:600}</style><ul class="bo" role="list"><li><strong>3 in 10</strong>homes sold went to a buyer with a tax bill in another state or country</li><li><strong>Over half</strong>of condos sold went to such a buyer</li></ul><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">North Carolina sends the most out-of-state buyers by far, then New York, Pennsylvania, New Jersey and Virginia. In the same building or subdivision, buyers with an out-of-state tax bill paid the same middle price as South Carolina buyers.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">More people keep coming. In the latest Census count, the Myrtle Beach metro grew faster than every U.S. metro but one. About 39 more people a day <a href="/buyers/relocating/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">moved into Horry County</a> from the rest of the country than moved out.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="what-can-go-wrong"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="What can go wrong">What can go wrong when buying in Myrtle Beach?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Six things here surprise buyers from other states. Each has a fix, and we handle most of them before you offer. The <a href="/buyers/common-mistakes/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">ten costliest buyer mistakes</a> are ranked on their own page.</p><style>main#main .br{list-style:none;margin:1.1rem 0 1.4rem;padding:0;display:grid;gap:.65rem;max-width:760px}@media (min-width:700px){main#main .br{grid-template-columns:repeat(2,minmax(0,1fr))}main#main .br li:last-child:nth-child(odd){grid-column:1/-1}}main#main .br li{margin:0;padding:1rem 1.1rem;border-radius:18px;background:rgba(255,255,255,.62);border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.8);box-shadow:0 10px 26px -20px rgba(28,32,40,.35);color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6}main#main .br h3{display:block;color:var(--navy)!important;font-family:var(--sans)!important;font-size:1.1rem!important;line-height:1.35;margin:0 0 .3rem!important;font-weight:700}main#main .br p{margin:0;max-width:none}main#main .br p.w{margin-top:.55rem;padding-top:.55rem;border-top:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.1);color:var(--navy);font-weight:600}main#main .br p.w a{font-weight:600}</style><ul class="br" role="list"><li><h3>Flood zone</h3><p>About 1 in 4 condos and 1 in 15 houses resold in Horry County in the past year sat in FEMA's high-risk flood zone. There, most mortgages require flood insurance. For a house you live in there, a federal flood policy cost a middle of about $1,070 a year.</p><p class="w">We find the zone before you offer, and look for <a href="/buyers/undisclosed-flooding/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">past flooding</a> too.</p></li><li><h3>Insurance</h3><p>The middle owner of a one-family house here pays about $2,000 a year for <a href="/buyers/coastal-insurance/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">home insurance</a>, about 40 percent more than in the rest of South Carolina. A condo owner pays about $800 for their own policy.</p><p class="w">We get a quote for the address before you offer, if you buy with us.</p></li><li><h3>The second-home tax rate</h3><p>If you never apply for the 4 percent rate, your home is taxed at 6 percent, as a second home is: about three times the tax (step 8).</p><p class="w">We work out the tax at both rates on any address you send.</p></li><li><h3>A low appraisal</h3><p>In our experience, low appraisals are rare here.</p><p class="w">When one comes in low, we send comparable sales with a request to look at the value again. Once, the value changed and the borrower brought less to closing.</p></li><li><h3>Nightly rentals</h3><p>Inside the City of Myrtle Beach, a rental under 90 days is legal only where the zoning allows it. About 3 in 4 condos are in such zones; about 96 in 100 houses are not. In North Myrtle Beach a <a href="/invest/str-rules/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">short-term rental</a> needs a business license, and for now there is no special zoning or permit process beyond it.</p><p class="w">We read the zoning and the HOA papers before you offer.</p></li><li><h3>Condos run like hotels</h3><p>Fannie Mae, which backs many ordinary home loans, will not back one in a building run like a hotel. Signs include a front desk, daily cleaning, or a rule that owners join the rental program. Buyers there need a <a href="/invest/condotel-financing/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">different loan</a> or cash.</p><p class="w">We read the building's rules before you offer, so you know which loans can work there.</p></li></ul></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="buying-in-myrtle-beach-faq"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Common questions</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3">Buying in Myrtle Beach FAQ</h2><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Is Myrtle Beach a buyer's market?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">For condos, yes: across Horry County, the condos for sale would take about 7.5 months to sell. For houses under $700,000, not yet.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Will home prices in Myrtle Beach go down?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">We do not forecast prices. Horry County house prices were about flat in the latest year, after four years of gains.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Do you need a lawyer to buy a house in South Carolina?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes. Every South Carolina home closing must be supervised by a South Carolina attorney. Step 5 says how you choose one.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What is a CL-100?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The termite letter, the Official South Carolina Wood Infestation Report. Only a licensed pest control applicator may issue it for a sale or a loan. It reports visible insect damage, active infestations, and moisture and decay under the house.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Can I rent my Myrtle Beach condo by the night?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Often, if it is inside the City of Myrtle Beach, where most condos sit in zones that allow it. The condo's own rules can still ban it.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">How much are property taxes in Myrtle Beach?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Inside the City of Myrtle Beach, on a house the county values at $350,000, about $1,410 a year if you live in it and apply. As a second home, about $5,350.</p><p style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:760px;margin-top:1.6rem"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Sources:</strong> <a href="https://acclaimweb.horrycounty.org/AcclaimWeb/search/SearchTypeDocType" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Horry County deed index</a>, <a href="https://trerc.tamu.edu/wp-content/uploads/files/PDFs/Articles/1897.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Texas A&M research center</a>, <a href="https://www.fhfa.gov/data/hpi" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FHFA house price index</a>, <a href="https://www.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest.html" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Census population estimates</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t40c057.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 40-57</a>, <a href="https://www.nar.realtor/the-facts/what-the-nar-settlement-means-for-home-buyers-and-sellers" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NAR</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t27c050.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 27-50</a>, <a href="https://www.sccourts.org/media/opinions/HTMLFiles/SC/25508.htm" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Doe v. McMaster</a>, <a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/ask-cfpb/what-is-a-loan-estimate-en-1995/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CFPB</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/coderegs/Chapter%2027.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Regulation 27-1085</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t12c043.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 12-43</a>, <a href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b4-2.1-03/ineligible-projects" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fannie Mae B4-2.1-03</a>, <a href="https://www.fema.gov/openfema-data-page/nfip-redacted-policies-v3" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OpenFEMA policies</a>. Supply: the Chapter3 listing feed, counted in total, against deeds recorded October 2025 to September 2026. Prices: FHFA, mid-2021 to mid-2025, then the year to mid-2026. Population: Census, the year to July 2025. Not legal or tax advice.</p><div class="c3ph-credits"><div class="c3ph-h