class="t">the federal planning life of elevator machinery</span></span></li></ul></div><p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.9rem;margin-bottom:1rem">By <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Devin Day</strong>, Operations Officer &middot; Reviewed by <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Tim Nash</strong>, Broker-in-Charge &middot; <span style="white-space:nowrap">Updated November 4, 2026</span></p><p class="detail-sub">A Myrtle Beach condo's roof and elevators wear out on a schedule. If its reserves cover the worn part, it can pay. If not, the owners do.</p></div></div><section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The short answer</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Only if it has saved enough. Its reserve fund is its savings for big repairs. Compare the cash in that fund with what its reserve study, a report on the building's big shared parts, says the fund should hold by now. That share is called percent funded. The higher it is, the lower the chance the owners get a special assessment, a one-time bill to each owner for a cost the fund cannot cover.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--navy);padding:3rem 0"><div class="wrap" style="text-align:center;max-width:720px;margin:0 auto"><p style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--ivory);margin-bottom:1rem">Send us the building. We read its reserve study, budget and balance sheet.</p><a class="btn btn-brass btn-lg" href="/contact/">Have us read the financials</a></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory);padding-top:2.5rem"><div class="wrap"><nav aria-label="On this page" style="margin-top:0;max-width:720px"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.3rem;font-weight:600">On this page</p><ul style="list-style:none;margin:0;padding:0"><li><a href="#what-reserves-are" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">What reserves are</a></li><li><a href="#south-carolina-rule" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">The South Carolina rule</a></li><li><a href="#how-much" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">How much is enough</a></li><li><a href="#example" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">An example</a></li><li><a href="#lenders" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">What lenders require</a></li><li><a href="#why-here" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Why it matters here</a></li><li><a href="#other-states" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Other states</a></li><li><a href="#reserve-study" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">No study, or an old one</a></li><li><a href="#what-we-read" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">What we read</a></li><li><a href="#reserve-questions" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Reserve questions</a></li></ul></nav></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="what-reserves-are"><di