ackdrop-filter:none!important;backdrop-filter:none!important;border-top:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.16)!important;padding:.8rem 0!important}main#main .c3-mb-life li:last-child{border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.16)!important}main#main figure.c3ph-hero{background:none!important;border:0!important;box-shadow:none!important;border-radius:0!important;-webkit-backdrop-filter:none!important;backdrop-filter:none!important;padding:0!important}main#main figure.c3ph-hero figcaption{padding:0!important}main#main .c3-mb-frame{background:none!important;border:0!important;box-shadow:none!important;border-radius:12px!important}main#main .c3-cc{background:none!important;border:0!important;box-shadow:none!important;border-radius:0!important;-webkit-backdrop-filter:none!important;backdrop-filter:none!important;padding:0!important}main#main .c3-cc thead th{border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.3)!important}main#main .c3-cc th:first-child,main#main .c3-cc td:first-child{padding-left:0}@media (max-width:39.999em){main#main .c3-cc tbody tr{background:none!important;border:0!important;box-shadow:none!important;border-radius:0!important;-webkit-backdrop-filter:none!important;backdrop-filter:none!important;border-top:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.16)!important;margin:0!important;padding:.7rem 0 .75rem!important}main#main .c3-cc tbody tr:last-child{border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.16)!important}main#main .c3-cc th,main#main .c3-cc td{padding-left:0!important;padding-right:0!important}}main#main .c3-mb-tiles{column-gap:2rem!important;row-gap:0!important}main#main .c3-mb-tile{background:none!important;border:0!important;box-shadow:none!important;border-radius:0!important;-webkit-backdrop-filter:none!important;backdrop-filter:none!important;border-top:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.16)!important;padding:.8rem 0 .4rem!important}main#main .c3-pc>div{background:none!important;border:0!important;box-shadow:none!important;border-radius:0!important;-webkit-backdrop-filter:none!important;backdrop-filter:none!important;border-top:2px solid var(--brass)!important;padding:.9rem 0 0!important}main#main .c3-pc{column-gap:2.5rem!important}main#main .c3-cc,main#main .c3-gchart,main#main figure.c3-mb-fig,main#main figure.c3ph-hero,main#main .c3-mb-life,main#main .c3-mb-tiles,main#main .c3-mb-sum,main#main .c3-pc{max-width:720px!important;box-sizing:border-box}@media (min-width:1180px){main#main .c3-mb-beside{display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,640px) minmax(0,1fr);grid-template-rows:auto 1fr;column-gap:2.5rem;align-items:start;margin:1.6rem 0 1.4rem}main#main .c3-mb-beside>*{grid-column:1}main#main .c3-mb-beside>figure.c3ph-hero{grid-column:2;grid-row:1/3;margin:.35rem 0 0!important;max-width:none!important}}</style><style>main#main .hx-strip:empty{display:none}main#main .hx-strip:not(:empty)+.hx-fallback{display:none}</style><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Myrtle Beach is a city of about 40,900 people on the ocean. About 15 percent more people live here than in 2020.</p><ul class="c3-mb-life"><li><strong>The Boardwalk.</strong> Downtown, the <a href="https://cityofmyrtlebeach.com/news_detail_T6_R986.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Boardwalk</a> is about 1.3 miles long, beside the ocean. It has a raised wooden boardwalk, a wide section for entertainment, and a winding walk along the ocean.</li><li><strong>A large hospital.</strong> Grand Strand Medical Center is inside the city, about a mile from the ocean. It has the region's only heart surgery program and a trauma center for the most serious injuries.</li><li><strong>The airport.</strong> Myrtle Beach International Airport is inside the city too, less than 2 miles from the ocean. It has nonstop flights to more than 50 cities.</li><li><strong>Golf and shops.</strong> Two golf clubs, Pine Lakes and The Dunes, are in the older neighborhoods described <a href="#older-neighborhoods" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">below</a>. The Market Common, on the south end, has shops, restaurants, a movie theater and two parks.</li></ul><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What is the off season like?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Quieter, not closed. In the off season, nothing closes except tourist attractions and water parks.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Hotels, restaurants and other leisure businesses in the county had about 30 percent more workers in July than in January last year.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="where-to-live"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Where to live">Where should you live in Myrtle Beach?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">In one of three parts of the city where 7 in 10 or more of the houses are their owners' main homes: Grande Dunes, The Market Common or the older neighborhoods near the golf clubs. Or in a condo, on the oceanfront or off it. For a house near $300,000, look at the <a href="#near-300k" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">two smaller neighborhoods</a> after part 3.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The numbers on the map show where each part is. The orange areas are where the city allows nightly rentals.</p><figure class="c3-mb-fig" id="rental-map"><div class="c3-mb-frame"><img src="/images/2026-11-a/submarkets-myrtle-beach/nightly-rental-zones-map.svg" width="760" height="717" alt="Map of the City of Myrtle Beach, a long strip along the ocean. Orange areas are where the city allows nightly rentals: most of the oceanfront, the Broadway at the Beach area and stretches of the main roads inland. Green areas are zoned for homes only, where it does not. Numbers mark 1 Grande Dunes at the north end, 2 The Market Common at the south end, 3 the older neighborhoods of houses north of downtown, and 4 the oceanfront condos. The airport is inside the city." loading="lazy" decoding="async"></div><figcaption><ul class="c3-mb-legend" aria-label="Map colors"><li><span class="c3-mb-sw" style="background:#c4783a"></span>Nightly rentals allowed</li><li><span class="c3-mb-sw" style="background:#5f8a6e"></span>Zoned for homes: no rentals under 90 days</li><li><span class="c3-mb-sw" style="background:#d9cfa6"></span>Planned neighborhoods such as Grande Dunes: not allowed</li><li><span class="c3-mb-sw" style="background:#d4d4cf"></span>Other zones: not allowed</li></ul>Where the City of Myrtle Beach allows nightly rentals: the city's own map of those zones, drawn by us on its current zoning lines. The numbers are the four parts of the city described below. Map data: City of Myrtle Beach zoning; Horry County GIS; U.S. Census Bureau TIGER/Line.<ol class="c3-mb-key" aria-label="The numbers on the map"><li><span class="c3-mb-num" aria-hidden="true">1</span><a href="#grande-dunes">Grande Dunes</a></li><li><span class="c3-mb-num" aria-hidden="true">2</span><a href="#market-common">The Market Common</a></li><li><span class="c3-mb-num" aria-hidden="true">3</span><a href="#older-neighborhoods">The older neighborhoods</a></li><li><span class="c3-mb-num" aria-hidden="true">4</span><a href="#oceanfront-condo">A condo on the oceanfront</a></li></ol></figcaption></figure><h3 id="grande-dunes" data-toc="Grande Dunes" class="c3-mb-h3" style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.3rem;color:var(--navy);margin:2rem 0 .6rem;line-height:1.3"><span class="c3-mb-num" aria-hidden="true">1</span><span>Grande Dunes: on the Waterway, with a beach club</span></h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Grande Dunes is a planned community on both sides of the Intracoastal Waterway, the inland water route behind the beach. Every homeowner gets a free household membership to the <a href="https://grandedunescommunities.sites.townsq.io/2" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Grande Dunes Ocean Club</a>. It has a large pool, a dining room, classes and clubs, and its own way onto the beach.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The community also has a public marina, a public golf course with holes along the water, a private members' golf course and a tennis club.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">About 4 in 5 houses here are their owners' main homes. Houses, villas and townhouses resold for about $880,000 in the middle, the highest of the parts in this guide.</p><div class="hx-strip" data-place="grande-dunes" data-name="Grande Dunes"></div><h3 id="market-common" data-toc="The Market Common" class="c3-mb-h3" style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.3rem;color:var(--navy);margin:2rem 0 .6rem;line-height:1.3"><span class="c3-mb-num" aria-hidden="true">2</span><span>The Market Common: shops, a lake and parks</span></h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The Market Common is a town center with shops, restaurants and a movie theater, and apartments above the stores. It was built on land that was the Myrtle Beach Air Force Base.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem"><a href="https://marketcommonmb.com/parks-playgrounds/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Grand Park and Valor Park</a> are next to the shops. They have a lake with a 1.1-mile path around it, sports fields, picnic areas, fishing spots and Savannah's Playground, built for children of all abilities.</p><div class="hx-strip" data-place="market-common" data-name="The Market Common"></div><div class="hx-fallback"><style>.c3ph-f{position:relative;margin:0;border-radius:6px;overflow:hidden;background:var(--ivory-2)}.c3ph-f img{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;object-fit:cover}.c3ph-cr{position:absolute;right:0;bottom:0;max-width:100%;padding:1.6rem .6rem .35rem 2.6rem;font:400 .625rem/1.3 var(--sans);letter-spacing:.01em;color:#fff;text-align:right;background:radial-gradient(farthest-side at 100% 100%,rgba(28,32,40,.72),rgba(28,32,40,.4) 55%,rgba(28,32,40,0));white-space:nowrap;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis;pointer-events:none}.c3ph-cr a{color:#fff;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:rgba(255,255,255,.5);text-underline-offset:2px;pointer-events:auto;padding:10px 0 6px}.c3ph-hero{margin:1.6rem 0 2rem}.c3ph-hero .c3ph-f{border-radius:8px}.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:4/3}@media (min-width:700px){.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:16/9}}.c3ph-hero figcaption{color:var(--muted);font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.5;margin-top:.55rem}</style><figure class="c3ph-hero" style="margin:1.6rem 0 1.8rem;max-width:680px;"><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/buyers/best-neighborhoods/market-common-valor-park-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/buyers/best-neighborhoods/market-common-valor-park-600w.webp 600w, /images/buyers/best-neighborhoods/market-common-valor-park-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 700px) 100vw, 680px" width="1200" height="900" alt="The Market Common from the air: streets of homes outlined in orange beside a lake, a dot labelled Valor Park at the edge of the shops, and ball fields and a lake labelled Grand Park" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/USCODE-2023-title17/html/USCODE-2023-title17-chap1-sec105.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="U.S. government aerial photo. License: Public domain" title="License: Public domain">U.S. government aerial photo</a></span></div><figcaption>The Market Common from the air. The homes outlined in orange are next to the shops and to the lake and ball fields of Grand Park.</figcaption></figure></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">About 2,500 houses are in the neighborhoods around it, among them Emmens Preserve, The Reserve, Sanctuary at Withers Preserve and Belle Harbor. About 6 in 7 are their owners' main homes, the highest share in this guide. Houses, villas and townhouses here resold for about $500,000 in the middle.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The city allows nightly rentals in the town center's zone, which includes a few hundred of these houses. It does not allow them in the other neighborhoods around it. Each neighborhood's HOA can still ban them, and we read its rules for you.</p><h3 id="older-neighborhoods" data-toc="The older neighborhoods" class="c3-mb-h3" style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.3rem;color:var(--navy);margin:2rem 0 .6rem;line-height:1.3"><span class="c3-mb-num" aria-hidden="true">3</span><span>The older neighborhoods: golf and streets by the ocean</span></h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">These neighborhoods are built around two golf clubs. <a href="https://pinelakes.com/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Pine Lakes Country Club</a> opened in 1927 as Myrtle Beach's first golf course. <a href="https://www.thedunesclub.com/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The Dunes Golf and Beach Club</a>, on the ocean, hosts a PGA Tour event, the Myrtle Beach Classic.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Myrtle Heights, Oak Park, Forest Dunes and Northwood are here too. On some streets, the houses face the beach across wide lawns.</p><div class="hx-strip" data-place="pine-lakes" data-name="Pine Lakes" data-head="Homes for sale near Pine Lakes now"></div><div class="hx-fallback"><style>.c3ph-f{position:relative;margin:0;border-radius:6px;overflow:hidden;background:var(--ivory-2)}.c3ph-f img{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;object-fit:cover}.c3ph-cr{position:absolute;right:0;bottom:0;max-width:100%;padding:1.6rem .6rem .35rem 2.6rem;font:400 .625rem/1.3 var(--sans);letter-spacing:.01em;color:#fff;text-align:right;background:radial-gradient(farthest-side at 100% 100%,rgba(28,32,40,.72),rgba(28,32,40,.4) 55%,rgba(28,32,40,0));white-space:nowrap;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis;pointer-events:none}.c3ph-cr a{color:#fff;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:rgba(255,255,255,.5);text-underline-offset:2px;pointer-events:auto;padding:10px 0 6px}.c3ph-hero{margin:1.6rem 0 2rem}.c3ph-hero .c3ph-f{border-radius:8px}.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:4/3}@media (min-width:700px){.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:16/9}}.c3ph-hero figcaption{color:var(--muted);font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.5;margin-top:.55rem}</style><figure class="c3ph-hero" style="margin:1.6rem 0 1.8rem;max-width:680px;"><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/2026-11-a/submarkets-myrtle-beach/oceanfront-houses-from-the-ocean-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/2026-11-a/submarkets-myrtle-beach/oceanfront-houses-from-the-ocean-600w.webp 600w, /images/2026-11-a/submarkets-myrtle-beach/oceanfront-houses-from-the-ocean-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 700px) 100vw, 680px" width="1200" height="782" alt="Two-story houses on wide green lawns behind the dunes, with wooden walkways to the beach and oak trees behind, seen from a plane over the ocean in Myrtle Beach" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://www.usa.gov/publicdomain/label/1.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Photo: U.S. Geological Survey. License: Public domain" title="License: Public domain">Photo: U.S. Geological Survey</a></span></div><figcaption>Houses on wide lawns facing the beach, on the older streets north of downtown Myrtle Beach.</figcaption></figure></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Nightly rentals are not allowed here, and about 7 in 10 houses are their owners' main homes. Houses resold for about $550,000 in the middle, with a wide range from street to street.</p><h3 id="near-300k" data-toc="Houses near $300,000" style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.3rem;color:var(--navy);margin:2rem 0 .6rem;line-height:1.3">Where are the houses near $300,000?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">In a few smaller neighborhoods. Houses in the city resold for about $540,000 in the middle in the past year, and only about 1 in 6 sold for $350,000 or less. Of the 869 homes for sale in the city at $300,000 or less, only 8 are houses. The rest are condos and townhouses.</p><style>.c3-cc{margin:1.4rem 0 1.8rem;max-width:960px}.c3-cc table{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:var(--read,1.0625rem);line-height:1.45;color:var(--muted)}.c3-cc caption{caption-side:top;text-align:left;color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;font-size:1em;line-height:1.4;padding:0 0 .7rem}.c3-cc th,.c3-cc td{padding:.6rem .7rem;text-align:left;vertical-align:top;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);overflow-wrap:break-word}.c3-cc thead th{color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;font-size:1em;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);vertical-align:bottom}.c3-cc tbody th{color:var(--navy);font-weight:600}.c3-cc .n{text-align:right;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}.c3-cc .i{display:inline-flex;vertical-align:-4px;margin-right:.45rem;color:var(--brass-ink)}.c3-cc tr.c3-self>*{background:#fff}@media (max-width:39.999em){.c3-cc table,.c3-cc tbody,.c3-cc tr,.c3-cc th,.c3-cc td{display:block;width:auto}.c3-cc caption{display:block}.c3-cc thead{position:absolute;width:1px;height:1px;overflow:hidden;clip:rect(0 0 0 0);white-space:nowrap}.c3-cc tbody tr{background:var(--ivory-2);border-top:3px solid var(--brass);margin:0 0 .8rem;padding:.5rem 0 .6rem}section[style*="background:var(--ivory-2)"] .c3-cc tbody tr{background:var(--ivory)}.c3-cc tr.c3-self{background:#fff!important;border:2px solid var(--brass)}.c3-cc tr.c3-self>*{background:transparent}.c3-cc th,.c3-cc td{border:0;padding:.2rem 1rem}.c3-cc tbody th{display:flex;align-items:center;font-size:1.1em;padding-bottom:.35rem}.c3-cc tbody th .i{flex:none;vertical-align:0}.c3-cc tbody th a{display:inline-block;padding:.5rem 0}.c3-cc .n{text-align:left}.c3-cc td[data-label]::before{content:attr(data-label) ": ";color:var(--navy);font-weight:500}}</style><div class="c3-cc"><table><caption>Where houses resold near $300,000 in the past year (12 to 25 sales each)</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Neighborhood</th><th scope="col">Middle resale price, houses</th><th scope="col">Houses in the high-risk flood zone</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Bent Oak Estates</th><td data-label="Middle resale price, houses">About $245,000</td><td data-label="Houses in the high-risk flood zone">Almost none</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Meridian</th><td data-label="Middle resale price, houses">About $350,000</td><td data-label="Houses in the high-risk flood zone">About 2 in 5</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Summit at Meridian</th><td data-label="Middle resale price, houses">About $420,000</td><td data-label="Houses in the high-risk flood zone">About 2 in 5</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Bent Oak Estates, off Pridgen Road, had the most house sales at $350,000 or less. It has about 150 houses, about a mile from the beach and from the airport. About 7 in 10 are their owners' main homes.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Meridian and Summit at Meridian, west of the airport, are one planned development of 513 houses. Of the city's zoning districts with 50 or more houses, it has the highest share in FEMA's high-risk flood zone. Nightly rentals are not allowed in any of the three.</p><h3 id="oceanfront-condo" data-toc="A condo on the oceanfront" class="c3-mb-h3" style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.3rem;color:var(--navy);margin:2rem 0 .6rem;line-height:1.3"><span class="c3-mb-num" aria-hidden="true">4</span><span>A condo on the oceanfront</span></h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">You can live in a condo too. Nearly every oceanfront condo building is where the city allows nightly rentals, so most of your neighbors there will be guests. Only about 1 in 18 condos there is someone's main home.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Off the oceanfront, where nightly rentals are not allowed, about 1 in 3 condos is its owner's main home. Those condos resold for about $270,000 in the middle, more than oceanfront condos, but there were only a few dozen sales. The <a href="#oceanfront-buildings" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">oceanfront buildings</a> are next.</p><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory-2);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Tour the part of the city you like</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">Tell us which part of Myrtle Beach fits you. We set up showings of the homes for sale there.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Book a showing in Myrtle Beach</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="nightly-rentals"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Nightly rentals">Can you rent a home by the night in Myrtle Beach?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Most condos, yes. Almost every house, no. Inside the City of Myrtle Beach, nightly rentals are legal only in the zones where the city allows them, and any stay shorter than 90 days counts.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Those zones include most of the oceanfront hotel and condo strip, the Broadway at the Beach area, stretches of the US 17 Bypass and US 501, and The Market Common's town center. They are the orange areas on the <a href="#rental-map" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">map</a>.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The city bans nightly rentals in every zone for homes, except one zone set aside for visitor stays. A house outside the allowed zones cannot be switched over to nightly rentals. Breaking the rule is a misdemeanor, with a fine, jail time or both for each violation.</p><style>.c3-cc{margin:1.4rem 0 1.8rem;max-width:960px}.c3-cc table{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:var(--read,1.0625rem);line-height:1.45;color:var(--muted)}.c3-cc caption{caption-side:top;text-align:left;color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;font-size:1em;line-height:1.4;padding:0 0 .7rem}.c3-cc th,.c3-cc td{padding:.6rem .7rem;text-align:left;vertical-align:top;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);overflow-wrap:break-word}.c3-cc thead th{color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;font-size:1em;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);vertical-align:bottom}.c3-cc tbody th{color:var(--navy);font-weight:600}.c3-cc .n{text-align:right;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}.c3-cc .i{display:inline-flex;vertical-align:-4px;margin-right:.45rem;color:var(--brass-ink)}.c3-cc tr.c3-self>*{background:#fff}@media (max-width:39.999em){.c3-cc table,.c3-cc tbody,.c3-cc tr,.c3-cc th,.c3-cc td{display:block;width:auto}.c3-cc caption{display:block}.c3-cc thead{position:absolute;width:1px;height:1px;overflow:hidden;clip:rect(0 0 0 0);white-space:nowrap}.c3-cc tbody tr{background:var(--ivory-2);border-top:3px solid var(--brass);margin:0 0 .8rem;padding:.5rem 0 .6rem}section[style*="background:var(--ivory-2)"] .c3-cc tbody tr{background:var(--ivory)}.c3-cc tr.c3-self{background:#fff!important;border:2px solid var(--brass)}.c3-cc tr.c3-self>*{background:transparent}.c3-cc th,.c3-cc td{border:0;padding:.2rem 1rem}.c3-cc tbody th{display:flex;align-items:center;font-size:1.1em;padding-bottom:.35rem}.c3-cc tbody th .i{flex:none;vertical-align:0}.c3-cc tbody th a{display:inline-block;padding:.5rem 0}.c3-cc .n{text-align:left}.c3-cc td[data-label]::before{content:attr(data-label) ": ";color:var(--navy);font-weight:500}}</style><div class="c3-cc"><table><caption>Which homes in the City of Myrtle Beach can rent by the night</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Kind of home</th><th scope="col">Homes in the city</th><th scope="col">Can rent by the night</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Condos</th><td data-label="Homes in the city">About 16,900</td><td data-label="Can rent by the night">About 3 in 4</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Houses</th><td data-label="Homes in the city">About 10,300</td><td data-label="Can rent by the night">About 4 in 100</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Villas and townhouses</th><td data-label="Homes in the city">About 850</td><td data-label="Can rent by the night">About 3 in 5</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="c3-mb-beside"><style>.c3ph-f{position:relative;margin:0;border-radius:6px;overflow:hidden;background:var(--ivory-2)}.c3ph-f img{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;object-fit:cover}.c3ph-cr{position:absolute;right:0;bottom:0;max-width:100%;padding:1.6rem .6rem .35rem 2.6rem;font:400 .625rem/1.3 var(--sans);letter-spacing:.01em;color:#fff;text-align:right;background:radial-gradient(farthest-side at 100% 100%,rgba(28,32,40,.72),rgba(28,32,40,.4) 55%,rgba(28,32,40,0));white-space:nowrap;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis;pointer-events:none}.c3ph-cr a{color:#fff;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:rgba(255,255,255,.5);text-underline-offset:2px;pointer-events:auto;padding:10px 0 6px}.c3ph-hero{margin:1.6rem 0 2rem}.c3ph-hero .c3ph-f{border-radius:8px}.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:4/3}@media (min-width:700px){.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:16/9}}.c3ph-hero figcaption{color:var(--muted);font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.5;margin-top:.55rem}</style><figure class="c3ph-hero" style="margin:1.6rem 0 1.8rem;max-width:680px;"><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/2026-11-a/submarkets-myrtle-beach/houses-and-towers-from-the-ocean-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/2026-11-a/submarkets-myrtle-beach/houses-and-towers-from-the-ocean-600w.webp 600w, /images/2026-11-a/submarkets-myrtle-beach/houses-and-towers-from-the-ocean-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 700px) 100vw, 680px" width="1200" height="782" alt="Older two-story houses on lawns along the beach at left, and two tall condo towers on the beach at right, with streets of houses under oak trees behind, seen from a plane over the ocean in Myrtle Beach" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://www.usa.gov/publicdomain/label/1.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Photo: U.S. Geological Survey. License: Public domain" title="License: Public domain">Photo: U.S. Geological Survey</a></span></div><figcaption>Houses and condo towers on the same beach in Myrtle Beach. The houses on the left are in a zone where nightly rentals are not allowed. The towers on the right are in one where they are.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The homes for sale today split the same way. Nearly every oceanfront condo for sale is in an allowed zone. Only about 1 in 5 houses and townhouses for sale in the city is.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Fewer than 30 houses in the zones for homes may still be rented by the night, because they were rented that way before the current zoning. No new ones can be added.</p></div><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">If a listing says short-term rentals are allowed, can you rent it nightly?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Not always. 35 houses and townhouses for sale in the city say so in the listing. We checked each of them against our map, and 3 are not in an allowed zone. Many HOAs also ban rentals of any length. We check the zone and the HOA's rules for any home you like, and we leave out the homes where your plan is not allowed.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What does a nightly rental need?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A <a href="https://www.cityofmyrtlebeach.com/careers/business/business_faq.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">city business license</a>. Every rental in the city needs one, short or long, and its fee is based on the rent you collect.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Guests pay about 13 percent in lodging taxes on top of each stay under 90 days. When a guest books through Airbnb, <a href="https://www.airbnb.com/help/article/2328" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Airbnb collects these taxes</a> for the state, the county and the city. On a booking you take yourself, you collect and pay them.</p><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Find out if you can rent a home by the night</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">Send us the address. Before you offer, we tell you if the city and its HOA let you rent it by the night.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Have us check a home's rental rules</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="oceanfront-buildings"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Oceanfront buildings">Which oceanfront condo buildings in Myrtle Beach sell the most?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">About 460 condos in oceanfront buildings inside the city resold in the past year. Nine buildings had the most sales, and their homes range from hotel-style studios to three-bedroom condos. For a one-bedroom on the oceanfront, plan on about $160,000, the middle asking price today.</p><style>.c3-cc{margin:1.4rem 0 1.8rem;max-width:960px}.c3-cc table{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:var(--read,1.0625rem);line-height:1.45;color:var(--muted)}.c3-cc caption{caption-side:top;text-align:left;color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;font-size:1em;line-height:1.4;padding:0 0 .7rem}.c3-cc th,.c3-cc td{padding:.6rem .7rem;text-align:left;vertical-align:top;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);overflow-wrap:break-word}.c3-cc thead th{color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;font-size:1em;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);vertical-align:bottom}.c3-cc tbody th{color:var(--navy);font-weight:600}.c3-cc .n{text-align:right;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}.c3-cc .i{display:inline-flex;vertical-align:-4px;margin-right:.45rem;color:var(--brass-ink)}.c3-cc tr.c3-self>*{background:#fff}@media (max-width:39.999em){.c3-cc table,.c3-cc tbody,.c3-cc tr,.c3-cc th,.c3-cc td{display:block;width:auto}.c3-cc caption{display:block}.c3-cc thead{position:absolute;width:1px;height:1px;overflow:hidden;clip:rect(0 0 0 0);white-space:nowrap}.c3-cc tbody tr{background:var(--ivory-2);border-top:3px solid var(--brass);margin:0 0 .8rem;padding:.5rem 0 .6rem}section[style*="background:var(--ivory-2)"] .c3-cc tbody tr{background:var(--ivory)}.c3-cc tr.c3-self{background:#fff!important;border:2px solid var(--brass)}.c3-cc tr.c3-self>*{background:transparent}.c3-cc th,.c3-cc td{border:0;padding:.2rem 1rem}.c3-cc tbody th{display:flex;align-items:center;font-size:1.1em;padding-bottom:.35rem}.c3-cc tbody th .i{flex:none;vertical-align:0}.c3-cc tbody th a{display:inline-block;padding:.5rem 0}.c3-cc .n{text-align:left}.c3-cc td[data-label]::before{content:attr(data-label) ": ";color:var(--navy);font-weight:500}}</style><div class="c3-cc"><table><caption>The nine oceanfront condo buildings in the city with the most resales in the past year</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Building</th><th scope="col">Middle resale price</th><th scope="col">What its homes for sale are now</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Dunes Village</th><td data-label="Middle resale price">About $269,000</td><td data-label="What its homes for sale are now">One- to three-bedrooms</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Long Bay Resort</th><td data-label="Middle resale price">About $170,000</td><td data-label="What its homes for sale are now">Studios to three-bedrooms</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Pinnacle Oceanfront Tower</th><td data-label="Middle resale price">About $150,000</td><td data-label="What its homes for sale are now">Studios and one-bedrooms</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Monterey Bay Suites</th><td data-label="Middle resale price">About $150,000</td><td data-label="What its homes for sale are now">One-bedrooms</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Caravelle Resort</th><td data-label="Middle resale price">About $145,000</td><td data-label="What its homes for sale are now">Mostly one-bedrooms</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Sand Dunes Oceanfront</th><td data-label="Middle resale price">About $131,000</td><td data-label="What its homes for sale are now">Studios to three-bedrooms</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Landmark Resort</th><td data-label="Middle resale price">About $102,000</td><td data-label="What its homes for sale are now">Studios and one-bedrooms</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Coral Beach</th><td data-label="Middle resale price">About $85,000</td><td data-label="What its homes for sale are now">Mostly one-bedrooms</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Tides/Driftwood/Oasis</th><td data-label="Middle resale price">About $53,000</td><td data-label="What its homes for sale are now">Studios</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="c3-mb-beside"><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The lowest prices are for studios of about 400 square feet at Tides, Driftwood and Oasis. Many of these buildings have a rental program for their owners.</p><style>.c3ph-f{position:relative;margin:0;border-radius:6px;overflow:hidden;background:var(--ivory-2)}.c3ph-f img{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;object-fit:cover}.c3ph-cr{position:absolute;right:0;bottom:0;max-width:100%;padding:1.6rem .6rem .35rem 2.6rem;font:400 .625rem/1.3 var(--sans);letter-spacing:.01em;color:#fff;text-align:right;background:radial-gradient(farthest-side at 100% 100%,rgba(28,32,40,.72),rgba(28,32,40,.4) 55%,rgba(28,32,40,0));white-space:nowrap;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis;pointer-events:none}.c3ph-cr a{color:#fff;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:rgba(255,255,255,.5);text-underline-offset:2px;pointer-events:auto;padding:10px 0 6px}.c3ph-hero{margin:1.6rem 0 2rem}.c3ph-hero .c3ph-f{border-radius:8px}.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:4/3}@media (min-width:700px){.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:16/9}}.c3ph-hero figcaption{color:var(--muted);font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.5;margin-top:.55rem}</style><figure class="c3ph-hero" style="margin:1.6rem 0 1.8rem;max-width:680px;"><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/2026-11-a/submarkets-myrtle-beach/oceanfront-towers-from-the-ocean-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/2026-11-a/submarkets-myrtle-beach/oceanfront-towers-from-the-ocean-600w.webp 600w, /images/2026-11-a/submarkets-myrtle-beach/oceanfront-towers-from-the-ocean-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 700px) 100vw, 680px" width="1200" height="782" alt="A row of oceanfront condo and hotel towers of 10 to 15 floors with pools in front, above a beach with umbrellas, seen from a plane over the ocean in Myrtle Beach" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://www.usa.gov/publicdomain/label/1.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Photo: U.S. Geological Survey. License: Public domain" title="License: Public domain">Photo: U.S. Geological Survey</a></span></div><figcaption>Oceanfront condo towers south of downtown Myrtle Beach, near three of the busiest buildings in the table above. This stretch is in a zone where nightly rentals are allowed.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Most buyers here live out of state. About 3 in 4 buyers of these oceanfront condos have their tax bill mailed outside South Carolina.</p></div><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Can you get a loan on an oceanfront condo?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes, but in many of these buildings not the most common kind. <a href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b4-2.1-03/ineligible-projects" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fannie Mae</a> will not back a loan in a building managed like a hotel: with a front desk, daily cleaning, or a rule that owners put their unit in the rental program.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Our preferred lender can tell you which loans work in the building you like. At closing, buyers usually also pay an HOA transfer fee and a few months of HOA dues in advance.</p><p class="c3-offer"><a class="c3-offer-pill" href="/contact/?place=Myrtle%20Beach">Get the oceanfront condos for sale in Myrtle Beach<span class="c3-offer-ar" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></span></a></p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="what-a-condo-earns"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="What a condo earns">Does an oceanfront condo in Myrtle Beach pay for itself?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">At typical figures, it covers its running costs and property tax, with about $8,100 a year left. That money must pay the HOA fee, a policy on the unit itself and any loan.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">For the example below, we used the middle asking price for a one-bedroom on the oceanfront, about $160,000. We used the income of a typical one-bedroom nightly rental in the Myrtle Beach market, not this unit's own.</p><div class="c3-mb-sum" role="group" aria-label="A year for an oceanfront one-bedroom rented by the night, at typical income and price"><span class="cap">A year for an oceanfront one-bedroom rented by the night, at typical income and price</span><ol><li><span>What a typical one-bedroom nightly rental took in for a year, counting listings open only part of the year</span><strong>About $17,000</strong></li><li><span>Running costs for an owner who runs it without a manager: the Airbnb fee, cleaning, utilities, supplies, repairs, a short-term rental policy and the permit</span><strong>About $6,500</strong></li><li><span>Property tax at the rate for second homes, on $160,000</span><strong>About $2,400</strong></li><li class="tot"><span>Left each year for the HOA fee, a policy on the unit itself and any loan</span><strong>About $8,100</strong></li></ol></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Whether the unit pays for itself depends on the building's HOA fee, which is different in every building.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Most of the year's rental income comes in summer</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The average listing takes in about 5 times as much in July as in January. June, July and August bring in almost half of a year's revenue.</p><figure class="c3-gchart c3-mb-fig"><span class="cap" style="display:block;color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;font-size:1.1em;line-height:1.35;margin:0 0 .5rem">What the average Myrtle Beach nightly rental takes in, by month</span><ul class="c3-mb-legend"><li><span class="c3-mb-sw" style="background:#c4783a"></span>June to August</li><li><span class="c3-mb-sw" style="background:#5c6b78;opacity:.55"></span>The other months</li></ul><svg class="c3-mb-chart" viewBox="0 0 360 232" width="100%" role="img" aria-label="Bar chart of what the average Myrtle Beach nightly rental takes in each month: January $1,340, February $1,680, March $2,530, April $2,800, May $3,270, June $6,070, July $6,800, August $4,400, September $2,550, October $2,150, November $1,620, December $1,590."><line x1="8" y1="196" x2="352" y2="196" stroke="#1c2028" stroke-opacity=".25"/><rect x="11" y="165" width="22.7" height="31" rx="4" fill="#5c6b78" fill-opacity=".55"/><text class="ct" x="22.3" y="218" text-anchor="middle">J</text><rect x="39.7" y="157.1" width="22.7" height="38.9" rx="4" fill="#5c6b78" fill-opacity=".55"/><text class="ct" x="51" y="218" text-anchor="middle">F</text><rect x="68.3" y="137.4" width="22.7" height="58.6" rx="4" fill="#5c6b78" fill-opacity=".55"/><text class="ct" x="79.7" y="218" text-anchor="middle">M</text><rect x="97" y="131.2" width="22.7" height="64.8" rx="4" fill="#5c6b78" fill-opacity=".55"/><text class="ct" x="108.3" y="218" text-anchor="middle">A</text><rect x="125.7" y="120.3" width="22.7" height="75.7" rx="4" fill="#5c6b78" fill-opacity=".55"/><text class="ct" x="137" y="218" text-anchor="middle">M</text><rect x="154.3" y="55.5" width="22.7" height="140.5" rx="4" fill="#c4783a" fill-opacity="1"/><text class="ct" x="165.7" y="218" text-anchor="middle">J</text><rect x="183" y="38.6" width="22.7" height="157.4" rx="4" fill="#c4783a" fill-opacity="1"/><text class="ct" x="194.3" y="218" text-anchor="middle">J</text><rect x="211.7" y="94.2" width="22.7" height="101.8" rx="4" fill="#c4783a" fill-opacity="1"/><text class="ct" x="223" y="218" text-anchor="middle">A</text><rect x="240.3" y="137" width="22.7" height="59" rx="4" fill="#5c6b78" fill-opacity=".55"/><text class="ct" x="251.7" y="218" text-anchor="middle">S</text><rect x="269" y="146.2" width="22.7" height="49.8" rx="4" fill="#5c6b78" fill-opacity=".55"/><text class="ct" x="280.3" y="218" text-anchor="middle">O</text><rect x="297.7" y="158.5" width="22.7" height="37.5" rx="4" fill="#5c6b78" fill-opacity=".55"/><text class="ct" x="309" y="218" text-anchor="middle">N</text><rect x="326.3" y="159.2" width="22.7" height="36.8" rx="4" fill="#5c6b78" fill-opacity=".55"/><text class="ct" x="337.7" y="218" text-anchor="middle">D</text><text class="cl" x="11" y="157" text-anchor="start" fill="#4a5763">$1,340</text><text class="cl" x="194.3" y="30.6" text-anchor="middle" fill="#8a5226">$6,800</text></svg><figcaption>Each month is the average of the past two years. The figures cover the whole Myrtle Beach market, which reaches past the city limits. Source: AirROI.</figcaption></figure><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">See if one oceanfront condo pays for itself</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">Send us the building. We get its HOA fee and run the figures in the table above for that condo.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Have us run the numbers on a condo</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="prices"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Prices">Are Myrtle Beach home prices dropping?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">For condos, yes. The typical condo value in Myrtle Beach is about 14 percent below its high in 2024, and it is still falling. For houses, no: the typical house value has stayed about the same since 2022.</p><figure class="c3-gchart c3-mb-fig"><span class="cap" style="display:block;color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;font-size:1.1em;line-height:1.35;margin:0 0 .5rem">Typical home value in Myrtle Beach, houses and condos</span><ul class="c3-mb-legend"><li><span class="c3-mb-line" style="background:#2f6f8f"></span>Houses</li><li><span class="c3-mb-line" style="background:#c4783a"></span>Condos</li></ul><svg class="c3-mb-chart" viewBox="0 0 360 250" width="100%" role="img" aria-label="Line chart of Zillow's typical home value in Myrtle Beach from 2019 to 2026. Single-family homes rose from about $243,000 to about $386,000 in 2022 and have held near $375,000. Condos rose from about $136,000 to about $239,000 in April 2024 and have fallen to about $207,000."><rect x="60" y="10" width="240" height="204" fill="rgba(255,255,255,.55)" rx="6"/><line x1="60" y1="146" x2="300" y2="146" stroke="#1c2028" stroke-opacity=".1"/><text class="ct" x="54" y="151" text-anchor="end">$200k</text><line x1="60" y1="78" x2="300" y2="78" stroke="#1c2028" stroke-opacity=".1"/><text class="ct" x="54" y="83" text-anchor="end">$300k</text><line x1="60" y1="10" x2="300" y2="10" stroke="#1c2028" stroke-opacity=".1"/><text class="ct" x="54" y="15" text-anchor="end">$400k</text><text class="ct" x="60" y="236" text-anchor="middle">2019</text><text class="ct" x="122.6" y="236" text-anchor="middle">2021</text><text class="ct" x="185.2" y="236" t